Updated on November 26, 2026

If you are building a mobile app, website, tool, or any other application that needs a backend, you know how much work waits on the server side. A Backend-as-a-Service (BaaS) takes most of it off your plate. It gives you pre-built infrastructure for data storage, user management, APIs, security, and database management, so you can focus on your product. Appwrite and Supabase are two popular open-source options, and while both cover the core of a backend, they approach it differently.

This article gives you a rundown of both platforms, compares them product by product, and ends with a summary table so you can see how each fits your needs.

For a wider **backend infrastructure** comparison that also includes Firebase and AWS Amplify, read [Backend as a service (BaaS)](/blog/post/backend-as-a-service). For a side-by-side table of every feature and price, see [Appwrite as a Supabase alternative](/alternative-to/supabase).

# Appwrite

[Appwrite](/) started in 2019 as an open-source project to make software development more accessible and enjoyable. It is an all-in-one development platform: authentication, databases, managed PostgreSQL, storage, functions, messaging, realtime, and web hosting, all in one Console. You can use [Appwrite Cloud](https://cloud.appwrite.io/) or self-host the same platform on your own servers. You can view Appwrite's source code on [GitHub](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite).

# Supabase

Supabase was founded in 2020 and positions itself as a Postgres development platform and an alternative to Firebase. Each Supabase project is a dedicated PostgreSQL database with authentication, storage, realtime, and edge functions built around it. You can use the Supabase Cloud platform or self-host. You can view Supabase's source code on [GitHub](https://github.com/supabase/supabase).

# Features compared

When you evaluate a BaaS, it helps to understand how each product serves your project. Here are the similarities and differences between Appwrite and Supabase.

## Authentication

Authentication verifies a user's identity so that only the right people can reach their private data. Methods include email and password, passwordless login by email or phone, and single sign-on (SSO). It is usually the first thing you build, and BaaS platforms simplify it with SDKs and APIs.

*Similarities:*

- Both offer user registration, login, and passwordless options.
- Both support email and password, OAuth providers such as Google and GitHub, and anonymous sessions.
- Both support multi-factor authentication.

*Differences:*

- Appwrite integrates with any external authentication system through [custom token login](/docs/products/auth/custom-token).
- Appwrite has [teams](/docs/products/auth/teams) with memberships and roles built in, plus labels, which makes multi-tenant apps and permissions simpler. In Supabase, you model teams yourself with tables and Row Level Security policies.
- Supabase offers SAML SSO for your own end users. SAML SSO for end users is coming soon to Appwrite.

## Databases

A database stores your application's data so it can be retrieved, queried, and updated efficiently. It is the core of almost every app, and it is where the two platforms differ most.

*Similarities:*

- Both offer managed PostgreSQL that you can query directly with SQL, psql, or any Postgres driver.
- Both run databases on dedicated compute, starting at $10/mo.
- Both provide a Console interface to create and manage tables, indexes, and relationships.

*Differences:*

- Appwrite gives you five database models in one project. [TablesDB](/docs/products/databases) runs on serverless or dedicated compute, and DocumentsDB, VectorsDB, [PostgreSQL](/docs/products/databases/postgresql), and MySQL run on dedicated compute. DocumentsDB, VectorsDB, PostgreSQL, and MySQL are in beta. Supabase gives you one PostgreSQL instance per project.
- Appwrite's [managed PostgreSQL](/products/postgres) runs PostgreSQL 18 (or 17) with connection pooling, up to five HA replicas, and up to 50 extensions, including pgvector and PostGIS.
- Point-in-time recovery on Appwrite adds 20% of the compute tier, with a restore window of 1 to 35 days. On Supabase, it costs $100/mo per 7 days and requires Small compute or larger.
- TablesDB, DocumentsDB, and VectorsDB come with SDKs, offset and cursor pagination, and [permissions](/docs/products/databases/tablesdb/permissions) as readable role strings on tables and rows, with no SQL required. Supabase protects rows with Row Level Security policies written in SQL. With Appwrite's managed PostgreSQL, you can still use Row Level Security when that is the model you want.
- Self-hosted Appwrite 2.0 runs on [PostgreSQL by default](/blog/post/appwrite-2-0-postgres-by-default), and you can choose MariaDB or MongoDB in the setup wizard instead.

## Storage

Storage manages the files in your project: images, videos, documents, and other binary data. It is a core requirement for apps such as social platforms or content tools.

*Similarities:*

- Both provide object storage for files, images, and other binary data.
- Both control access to files with permissions.

*Differences:*

- Appwrite offers file previews and an [image transformation API](/docs/products/storage/images) for resizing, cropping, reformatting, and compression. Both platforms include image transformations on paid plans.
- Appwrite image transformations can serve each image in the best format the browser supports, automatically.
- Appwrite [buckets](/docs/products/storage/buckets) can encrypt and compress files with gzip or zstd, enabled per bucket in its settings.

