## Functions

Functions are self-contained pieces of code that run on demand. They let you extend your backend with custom logic without managing servers.

*Similarities:*

- Both provide serverless functions that run on demand.
- Both support cron schedules and event triggers, such as file uploads or database changes.

*Differences:*

- Appwrite supports [13+ function runtimes](/docs/products/functions/runtimes), including Node.js, Python, Go, Dart, and PHP. Supabase Edge Functions run TypeScript on a Deno-compatible runtime.
- Appwrite builds its runtimes in-house through [Open Runtimes](https://github.com/open-runtimes/open-runtimes), so it can add languages and versions quickly.
- Appwrite offers function templates that deploy in a few clicks, such as Discord bots, Stripe payments, and AI integrations.

## Realtime

Realtime delivers updates to your app as soon as they happen, which powers features such as chat, live dashboards, and collaboration. Both platforms make this quick to build.

*Similarities:*

- Both deliver realtime updates over WebSockets.
- Both support broadcasting changes to large audiences.
- Both offer client libraries in several languages.

*Differences:*

- Appwrite realtime covers every service: rows, files, function executions, sessions, and teams. Supabase realtime covers Postgres changes, broadcast, and presence.
- Supabase offers presence tracking across users out of the box.

## Messaging

Messaging lets you reach your users directly through push notifications, SMS, and email. Appwrite has it built in. Supabase does not offer messaging, so you integrate external providers yourself.

*Similarities:*

- In both, you can combine realtime and databases to build a chat application.
- In both, you can call external SMS and email providers from functions.

*Differences:*

- [Appwrite Messaging](/docs/products/messaging) connects to 12 providers for email, SMS, and push notifications, with topics, scheduling, and message previews. Supabase offers none of these.

## Self-hosting

Self-hosting means running the platform on your own infrastructure instead of a managed cloud. It gives you full control over your data and where it lives. Both Appwrite and Supabase are open source, so both can be self-hosted.

*Similarities:*

- Both self-host with Docker.

*Differences:*

- Appwrite installs with a [single command](/docs/advanced/self-hosting) and a setup wizard, and runs the same APIs, SDKs, and Console as Appwrite Cloud. Supabase self-hosting uses Docker Compose, and you configure each component yourself.
- One self-hosted Appwrite instance runs many projects. A self-hosted Supabase instance runs one project.
- Supabase self-hosting is community supported.

## Hosting

Appwrite includes web hosting, so you can develop, deploy, and scale your whole application from one platform. Supabase focuses on the backend and does not host frontends.

*Differences:*

- [Appwrite Sites](/docs/products/sites) hosts static and server-side rendered apps, with Git deployments and preview builds. With Supabase, you pair the backend with a separate frontend host such as Vercel or Netlify.
- With Appwrite, your frontend, backend, domains, deployments, and monitoring live in one Console. With Supabase, you manage several services and dashboards.

## Support

Every developer product needs a good support system. Both Appwrite and Supabase offer ways to get help and share feedback.

*Similarities*:

- Both offer comprehensive documentation and community forums.
- Both offer paid support plans with priority email assistance.
- Both have active open-source communities.

*Differences*:

- The Appwrite community on [Discord](https://appwrite.io/discord) is very active and responds quickly.
- Supabase offers a higher tier of support at a higher price.

# Conclusion

Both Appwrite and Supabase let you focus on building instead of operating backend systems, and both give you managed PostgreSQL. The difference is how much of your stack each one covers.

Supabase is a strong choice when your project is centered on Postgres and you need little else from the platform. It also fits if you rely on PostgREST for an auto-generated REST API, offer SAML SSO to your own end users, or use an AI app builder with a one-click Supabase integration.

Appwrite covers more of your stack in one place. You get managed PostgreSQL when you want raw SQL, and TablesDB, DocumentsDB, and VectorsDB with SDKs and readable permissions when you don't. Auth, storage, functions in 13+ runtimes, messaging, realtime, and web hosting come with it. Whether you use Appwrite Cloud or self-host, you get the same platform and the same APIs.

Here's a table that compares Appwrite and Supabase:

| Feature | Appwrite | Supabase |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Database models | 5: TablesDB, DocumentsDB, VectorsDB, PostgreSQL, MySQL | 1: PostgreSQL |
| Managed PostgreSQL | Yes, PostgreSQL 18 on dedicated compute, in beta | Yes |
| Serverless or dedicated databases | Serverless TablesDB, or dedicated compute for any engine | One dedicated instance per project |
| Permissions | Readable role strings on tables and rows, no SQL required | Row Level Security policies written in SQL |
| Function runtimes | 13+ languages | TypeScript on a Deno-compatible runtime |
| Function templates | Yes, deploy in a few clicks | No |
| Messaging | 12 providers for email, SMS, and push | None |
| Web hosting | Yes, static and SSR with Appwrite Sites | No |
| Self-hosting | One Docker install, many projects per instance | Docker Compose, one project per instance |
| Pro plan | From $25/mo, 200K MAU, 2TB bandwidth | From $25/mo, 100K MAU, 250GB bandwidth |

